  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1059 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nThe current surface analysis shows Wisconsin positioned between \nsurface high pressure building over the northern Plains and a cold\nfront shifting across the eastern Great Lakes. This high pressure\nsystem will slowly settle over the western Great Lakes over the\nnext 24 hours. Broad subsidence and light winds will yield \ngenerally quiet weather across north- central and northeast \nWisconsin through Friday. \n\nGusty Northwest Winds Thursday: A tight surface pressure gradient\ncombined with steep boundary layer lapse rates will drive gusty \nnorthwest winds across the region again on Thursday. Momentum \ntransfer from 850mb wind profiles around 25 to 30 knots will \nsupport surface wind gusts of 25 to 30 mph across central and \nnortheast Wisconsin during Thursday afternoon. Winds will quickly \ndecouple and diminish Thursday evening as the surface high \npressure center settles directly over Wisconsin. \n\nPatchy Frost Late Thursday Night: High pressure centered over \nWisconsin Thursday night will promote decoupling winds which is \nfavorable for frost. However, some indications of mid and high \nclouds overhead for large portions of the night. This leads to \nlower confidence for issuing a Frost Advisory despite NBM guidance\nshowing a 50 to 90 percent probability of temperatures falling \nbelow 37 degrees across the Northwoods, with Land O Lakes and \nCrandon reaching near the freezing mark. Will let the next shift \ntake another look before issuing a Frost Advisory across Vilas, \nOneida, Lincoln, Forest, Florence, and northern Marinette \ncounties. High confidence exists that max temperatures recover \nquickly into the upper 60s on Friday afternoon. \n\nUnseasonable Weekend Heat And Strong Winds: \nAs surface high pressure shifts to the East Coast, an intense \nupper-level ridge will build across the western Great Lakes, \ntriggering robust southerly return flow. Daytime high temperatures\non Saturday and Sunday will climb into the upper 70s and lower \n80s across inland Wisconsin, approaching daily record high values \nat some locations. Tightening regional pressure gradients will \ndrive gusty southerly winds of 30 to 40 mph, with NBM \nprobabilistic guidance indicating a 40 to 60 percent probability \nof gusts exceeding 35 mph on Saturday. \n\nEarly Week Cold Front And Cool Down: A cold front associated with\na northern stream trough will traverse Wisconsin Sunday night \ninto Monday. Rain chances remain on track to develop post frontal \non Monday afternoon through Monday night. Mid-level frontogenesis \nwill increase 24-hour precipitation probabilities to 40 to 50 \npercent area- wide, though dynamic forcing remains progressive. \nNBM probabilistic guidance indicates a 20 to 30 percent chance of \n24-hour liquid accumulations exceeding 0.25 inches, with expected \nrainfall totals generally under a quarter inch. Post-frontal cold \nair advection will usher in a significantly cooler airmass for \nTuesday and Wednesday, dropping daytime high temperatures back \ninto the lower to mid-50s under partly sunny skies.",
